  "id": "ch2",
  "title": "THE CORAL CAUSEWAY",
  "objPage": "S3A0",
  "preset": "coral",
  "notes": [
    "COHERENCE (2026-10-22): ch2 is one place, the tidal causeway, from end to end. The drive (a0 = S1-S3, a2 = S6) is the HELL ROAD loop E3a0 15872-19712 (the ROM's own repeat, period 3840 = 0xF00) under the coral palette; a1 (S4-S5, on foot at the old toll post) is the same causeway: E3a0 6144-6656 walked back and forth (every leg <= 512 px, leftward legs mirrored) under the same palette, with the same sea along its far edge and the same far layer, so stopping the car does not change the place.",
    "The causeway: the far edge of the HELL ROAD ground (y 128-160) is sea in every column: E6a1 open-water cells (E6a1 2688-3008, h 304-336, palette row 20, dimmed to the dusk), every other 320-px copy mirrored so the copies meet on mirror joins; the HELL ROAD mesas now stand in the sea as coral stacks.",
    "2-6 THE RISING TIDE (boss: BREAKWATER, the harnessed ankylosaur towing freight): the tide is one row higher and has crossed the far edge of the road (water at y 112-176; the drive band stays 161-224). The low-tide flats carry the wreck of an old pier: rope-wound pilings (the E7a2 cable-wound pillar, lifted, behind the sprites) stand in the water every 640 px, where abandoned freight was once moored. The loop's wrap copy (x 3840-4224) repeats x 0-384 exactly, patches included.",
    "Gate 4 takes the loop exemption (user question 5 default) only for the boss window: the loop content repeats into the original HELL ROAD boss window at 32752 (reported as a warning); every loop column is patched, so no verbatim run remains.",
    "The coral preset's ops are all row-role ops; every area gives explicit row ops instead (E3a0 scroll2 row 2 = the sand: hue -12 and 16% toward #d98a8a, coral flats; scroll3 row 4 = the mesas: 30% toward #d98a8a, coral stacks; row 5 = the dusk sky: 18% toward #3a5a9a, sea light), so no piece keeps the original palette.",
    "a1 toll post: the portal timber of E6a0 (864-896, rows 9/8/3/2 lifted, dressing) stands as toll posts at the section join and at the barrier."
  ],
